{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_femr6cxcjf4urzqphx6vesswegcwhq4gnvixqopz4dtpdpd5kawa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four completion gates pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single 32-line file exporting one symbol: `void tfm(float *dest, const float *src, int count)` — a per-element eigenvalue-ish transform over triples. No features, no `#ifdef`, no error returns, no asserts.\n\n## Phase A artifacts (`translation/`)\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C exports exactly `{tfm}`; Rust exports `{tfm}`. Diff empty, 0 missing. No module was left untranslated, no stubs added.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 26 rows, derived by grepping the C for every rejection. The C has *no* `return`/`assert`/NULL-check, so its whole rejection surface is the loop guard `i < count`, the clamp `(0 > sqd) ? 0 : sqd`, and IEEE's implicit rejections.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 44 rows over the axes the C actually branches on (branch selection, clamp regime, count shape, IEEE class per lane, buffer overlap/alignment, call composition).\n- **`VERIFICATION.md`** — the completion gate with evidence.\n\nRow→test mapping is 1:1 and machine-checked (44↔44, 26↔26, verified by `diff`).\n\n## Results: 83 tests, 25 matrix steps, 0 failures\n\nEverything goes through `dlopen`/`dlsym`, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrappers are under test.\n\n**One real divergence found and fixed.** NULL pointer with `count > 0`: C gives `SIGSEGV`, the `release` Rust `.so` gave `SIGSEGV`, but the **`dev` `.so` gave `SIGABRT`** — rustc's UB sanitizer converted the null deref into a panic. Fixed on the Rust side via `[profile.dev] debug-assertions = false` (keeping `overflow-checks = true`). The shipped object was always right; only the debug one was wrong, so single-profile testing would have missed it.\n\nWhile chasing that I hit a second process bug worth flagging: **`cargo test` does not rebuild the cdylib**, because the tests `dlopen` it rather than link it. A stale `.so` was silently \"passing\" and briefly masked the above. There's now a staleness guard test, and `run_all.sh` always builds first.\n\n## Things I checked rather than assumed\n\n- **Two conditions in my own tables turned out unreachable.** `sqd == -0.0f` (0 hits over 24³ alphabet + 400k randoms — provably impossible since `dxy_term` is a square), and the `quiet()` in `fsqrt` (`sqrtf`'s argument can never be a signalling NaN). Both now *assert* unreachability plus test the reachable neighbours, rather than passing vacuously.\n- **`sqd < 0` is reachable but only through rounding** (mathematically it's `(dy2-dx2)² + 4dxy² ≥ 0`). I derived a constructor from near-equal operands `1 + p·2⁻²³`, giving 400 clamp-taken hits across both branches — otherwise that branch would never have been exercised.\n- **Mutation testing (7 mutations)** to confirm the suite can fail. 5 caught. M7 was a deliberate benign control (`2.0f*dx2` → `dx2+dx2`, provably identical) and correctly survived. M5 was the `fsqrt` quieting — the only non-control survivor, now backed by a proof and a regression test.\n- **Optimization-level robustness.** The C is *not self-consistent* across `-O` levels, so I quantified it: for **all NaN-free inputs the Rust is bit-exact at every conforming `-O` level**, and wherever it differs on NaN inputs, the canonical C differs identically — i.e. GCC's own operand-ordering instability, not a translation defect.\n- **Independent ABI check** (`probe/abi_probe.c`): a native C program linked *directly* against each `.so` produces 22,821 byte-identical output lines for both Rust profiles.\n\n`c_src/` was never modified (md5s verified); `src/lib.rs` is byte-identical to how I found it. `cargo check --tests` is warning-clean."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":211,"cache_read_input_tokens":222020,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":211,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"63390445-8e5a-4e75-97aa-9da9e34afbef","uuid":"75ed578c-f15c-476c-9123-ffffa920a8ab","timestamp":"2026-08-27T02:00:52.880Z"}
